"A two-phase commit diagram shows participants preparing a distributed transaction before a coordinator decides commit or abort.\n\nUse when: Explain prepared transaction states and coordinator failure windows. Review atomic commitment across supported transaction participants.\n\nAvoid when: The workflow requires business compensation rather than atomic commitment.\n\nVisual grammar: Coordinator and participant lanes separate protocol roles. Prepare and decision phases are explicitly labeled. Vote, durable decision, and acknowledgment messages show the commitment protocol.\n\nBuild: Choose a supported protocol implementation and failure assumptions. Draw prepare requests and participant votes. Show durable decision propagation and the abort branch. Add the uncertain prepared-state interval without crowding protocol messages.\n\nCheck: Commit follows successful preparation by all required participants. A prepared participant cannot arbitrarily forget its promise. Coordinator loss and blocking behavior follow the selected implementation. Phase boundaries and failure notes remain clear of messages.\n\nPitfalls: Confusing prepare with a reversible business reservation. Assuming a timeout always permits a participant to abort independently.\n\nExample: A coordinator prepares updates in two resource managers, records the decision, then sends commit to both.\n\nAlternatives: Saga orchestration diagram, Transaction schedule diagram."
